@@ -0,0 +1,374 @@
+# `syrvis share declare|list` + `syrvis user declare|list` — the NAS data-plane seam verbs
+
+Status: implemented (this tree). Ratified 2026-08-10. Lineage: home-tech
+design/42 D6 ("runbook-first, never blocked on the feature — the verbs
+RE-DECLARE what the runbook built"), design/45 §4/§8 (the share registry + ACL
+matrix; §11 lists these verbs as deferred-never-gating), design/51 D5 (the
+platform track owns this — *not* a tofu provider; also ratifies the additive
+`python -m syrviscore.seam.gen verbs --json` registry dump).
+
+This document is the feature spec: the decisions, the frozen payload schemas,
+the registered argv shapes, the blast-radius guards, and what is deliberately
+out of scope. The deployment-repo context it serves (share registry yaml,
+principal model, verify checks) stays in the deployment repo; nothing here
+names a concrete estate.
+
+---
+
+## 1. What these verbs are
+
+A deployment repo keeps a share registry (declared shared folders + a
+principal→`rw|ro` access matrix) and a human-account posture (local DSM users,
+`nologin` shells, `users`-only group membership). Until now the only
+reconciler was a hand runbook driven through the DSM web UI. These verbs make
+the platform the reconciler, on the platform's own converge philosophy:
+
+- `syrvis share declare` / `syrvis user declare` — read a **resolved JSON
+  payload on STDIN**, validate it against a frozen schema, and idempotently
+  converge the DSM state to it (create if missing, assert flags/ACLs/posture),
+  reporting what changed as JSON. `--dry-run` plans without mutating.
+- `syrvis share list` / `syrvis user list` — read-only JSON dumps of current
+  DSM share/user state, for drift comparison by deployment-side checks (the
+  registry's "no undeclared shares" check, ACL audits, account-posture checks).
+- `syrvis seam verbs` (+ `python -m syrviscore.seam.gen verbs`) — the
+  registered-verb catalog as JSON, so external tooling can verify a deployed
+  seam's contract instead of hardcoding call shapes (§7).
+
+Like `apply`/`deploy`, the declares are **stdin verbs**: the payload travels
+sops→memory→ssh-stdin and never touches argv/ps/logs. The seam argv is fixed
+(`share declare --json`), so nothing estate-specific ever crosses the shim.
+
+## 2. Input model — resolved payloads, not registry files
+
+The seam accepts registered argv only, and the payload follows the
+`syrvis apply` pattern: the deployment repo renders its registry
+(`shares.d/*.yaml` + its principal model) into ONE resolved, self-contained
+JSON document client-side and streams it:
+
+    <shares.json> | ssh <operator> -- sudo -n .../syrvis share declare --json
+
+**Resolved, not raw**: the registry yaml carries estate-level concepts the
+platform must not learn (dataset links, GC classes, sensitivity taxonomy,
+protocol policy, lint rules 11–15). The client-side wrapper flattens those to
+the DSM-level intent below; SyrvisCore validates and converges exactly that.
+This mirrors how `apply` receives a rendered instance bundle, not
+deployment.yaml.
+
+### 2.1 `syrvis-shares/v1`
+
+```json
+{
+  "apiVersion": "syrvis-shares/v1",
+  "shares": {
+    "Books": {
+      "volume": "/volume5",
+      "description": "Book-content resting share",
+      "hide_unauthorized": true,
+      "recycle_bin": false,
+      "encrypted": false,
+      "acl": {"kevin": "rw", "lisa": "rw", "syrvis-reader": "ro"}
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+- The mapping key is the DSM share name (1–32 chars, no control chars, no
+  `/` or `\`, no leading/trailing space — DSM's own naming envelope; spaces
+  are legal, e.g. `Secure Documents`).
+- `volume`: `^/volume[0-9]{1,2}$`. The share path is derived as
+  `<volume>/<name>` — the payload never carries a free path.
+- `description`: optional, single-line, ≤ 128 chars.
+- `hide_unauthorized`: DSM's "hide sub-folders and files from users without
+  permissions" (adv. permission `hide_unreadable`). Required — the registry's
+  default-deny posture wants it explicit, not defaulted.
+- `recycle_bin`: required, same reasoning.
+- `encrypted`: required. **Assert-only** — see the encryption guard (§5.3).
+- `acl`: principal-slug → `rw | ro | none`. Principals match the platform
+  name charset (`[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}`). `none` is the *named* removal
+  form: to revoke a principal you must name it — the verb never removes a
+  grant it wasn't told about (§5.2). Absent principals are reported as
+  unmanaged drift, untouched.
+
+### 2.2 `syrvis-users/v1`
+
+```json
+{
+  "apiVersion": "syrvis-users/v1",
+  "users": {
+    "kevin": {
+      "full_name": "Kevin",
+      "shell": "/sbin/nologin",
+      "groups": ["users"]
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+- The mapping key is the local DSM username (platform name charset).
+- `full_name`: optional; used at create time (synouser's gecos field) and
+  reported as drift afterwards — never converged (a gecos rewrite is not
+  worth a user-database regeneration, see §6.1).
+- `shell`: `/sbin/nologin | /bin/sh` — the two postures the estate uses
+  (daily humans are nologin; seam accounts are /bin/sh). Converged by a
+  surgical, count-checked `/etc/passwd` rewrite (the provision script's
+  proven pattern), never a whole-file template.
+- `groups`: asserted memberships, **additive-only** (membership in a group
+  not listed is reported, never removed). `administrators` and `docker` are
+  refused outright — DSM's docker group is root-equivalent, and admin
+  membership is exactly what the human-identity design forbids; both stay
+  OWNER-DECISION through the DSM UI.
+
+### 2.3 Frozen schemas
+
+Both payload schemas are exported as importable constants
+(`syrviscore.shares.SHARES_PAYLOAD_SCHEMA`,
+`syrviscore.users.USERS_PAYLOAD_SCHEMA` — JSON-Schema-shaped documents) and
+the validators derive their allowed keys and enums *from* those constants, so
+code and schema cannot diverge. `tests/test_payload_schemas.py` pins the
+sha256 of each schema's canonical JSON: an accidental edit fails the test;
+a deliberate contract change updates the digest **and bumps the apiVersion**.
+Validation itself stays hand-rolled (import-light, Python 3.8, no jsonschema
+dependency on the DSM CLI) — the constant is the contract artifact, the
+validator is the enforcement.
+
+## 3. Idempotence + drift reporting
+
+`declare` is compare-then-converge, safe to re-run:
+
+1. **Plan**: read current DSM state for *only the payload-named* shares/users;
+   compute per-item actions (`create | update | unchanged`) and per-field
+   changes.
+2. **Guard**: any blast-radius violation (§5) aborts the whole payload
+   *before any mutation* — a payload is applied all-or-not-at-all at the
+   validation/guard layer (mirrors `apply`).
+3. **Converge**: execute only the differing steps, per item.
+4. **Re-read**: after mutating, read the state back and report **residual**
+   drift honestly (`converged: false` + the still-different fields). This is
+   deliberate: the DSM backend argv forms are asserted against DSM's API, and
+   a form that silently no-ops must surface as residual drift, never as a
+   false "changed" report.
+
+Report shape (both planes; `--json` contract):
+
+```json
+{
+  "dry_run": false,
+  "shares": {
+    "Books": {
+      "action": "update",
+      "changes": {"recycle_bin": {"from": true, "to": false}},
+      "acl_changes": {"lisa": {"from": null, "to": "rw"}},
+      "unmanaged_acl": ["cerebrate"],
+      "converged": true,
+      "errors": []
+    }
+  },
+  "ok": true
+}
+```
+
+Per-item backend failures land in that item's `errors` and flip top-level
+`ok` to false (exit 1); other items still converge — the reconcile-style
+aggregation, and a re-run picks up where it failed. `--dry-run` performs
+steps 1–2 only and reports the same shape with `"dry_run": true`.
+
+`list` verbs return the raw current state (shares: name, path, description,
+flag posture, encryption + mount state, permission matrix; users: name, uid,
+shell, full name, groups, expiry state) — no registry knowledge, no
+filtering: the deployment side owns the comparison.
+
+## 4. Registered argv shapes (seam registry entries)
+
+Following the `apply`/`apply_plan` precedent — the shim matches exact argv
+shapes, so dry-run is its own registered form:
+
+| id | argv (after the binary) | tier |
+|---|---|---|
+| `share_declare_plan` | `share declare --dry-run --json` | sudo + read-only |
+| `share_declare` | `share declare --json` | sudo, converge (non-destructive) |
+| `share_list` | `share list --json` | sudo + read-only |
+| `user_declare_plan` | `user declare --dry-run --json` | sudo + read-only |
+| `user_declare` | `user declare --json` | sudo, converge (non-destructive) |
+| `user_list` | `user list --json` | sudo + read-only |
+| `seam_verbs` | `seam verbs --json` | read-only, no sudo |
+
+- No slots, no positionals: the payload names its targets, so nothing
+  user-chosen crosses the argv (the strongest possible shim shape — literal
+  tokens only). No new slot kinds, no shim predicate changes.
+- **sudo on the lists** mirrors `vm_list`/`export`: the DSM backends
+  (`synowebapi`, `/etc/passwd` posture reads under the operator account,
+  synouser-owned state) are root-only; the CLI performs no privileged
+  *mutation* on those paths.
+- **Converge tier, no confirmation token**: the declares are idempotent
+  intent, structurally unable to delete or disable (§5) — the same class as
+  `apply`/`service_declare`, not `reconcile --prune`.
+- The declares are **stdin writers → script-only, never MCP tools** (seam
+  contract §1). The lists and `seam verbs` are safe candidates for future MCP
+  tools; deliberately not added in this change.
+- `Command` gains an additive `stdin` field naming the wire format a verb
+  reads (`"syrvis-shares/v1"`, `"text"`, …, default `None`) so the catalog
+  (§7) documents the stdin contract. It does not affect sudoers/shim
+  rendering.
+
+**Deployment pickup**: nothing on a live NAS changes until a release carrying
+these verbs is installed and `syrvisctl activate` runs — its seam re-sync
+(auto_seam_update) re-renders the sudoers allowlist + forced-command shim from
+this registry, at which point the seven shapes above become invocable. A
+rollback narrows the seam back. The committed
+`packages/syrviscore-mcp/deploy/` sudoers/shim artifacts are regenerated in
+this change so the G18 drift test holds.
+
+## 5. Blast-radius guards (invariants, each with tests)
+
+1. **Payload scope**: the verb reads and mutates only shares/users *named in
+   the payload*. It never enumerates-and-converges, never prunes. (The
+   read-only `list` verbs enumerate — that is their job.)
+2. **No destructive transitions, structurally**: there is no delete/disable
+   code path. A share is never deleted, renamed, or moved; a user is never
+   deleted, disabled, or expired; a group membership is never removed; an ACL
+   grant is only removed for a principal the payload explicitly names with
+   `"none"`. Deletion/disable stay OWNER-DECISION (DSM UI / break-glass),
+   per the house rule that tools print evidence, never destroy.
+3. **Encryption is create-time and human**: DSM share encryption (eCryptfs +
+   Key Manager) is a create-time property, and headless creation would put
+   key material on a process argv and Key Manager enrolment out of reach —
+   so `share declare` **refuses to create** a share declared
+   `"encrypted": true` (error `share_encrypted_create_unsupported`, pointing
+   at the deployment's runbook). For an *existing* encrypted share it
+   asserts posture only (flags + ACL) and reports mount state; it never
+   touches keys, never mounts/unmounts (`synoshare --enc_mount` stays a
+   runbook/secure-share concern). Declaring `"encrypted": false` against an
+   encrypted share (or vice versa on an existing plain share) is a
+   contradiction, not drift to fix: guard error, nothing mutated.
+4. **Privileged groups refused**: `groups` containing `administrators` or
+   `docker` fails validation (docker == root-equivalent on DSM; the seam must
+   not be able to mint an escalation).
+5. **Password boundary**: no password field exists in the schema; no code
+   path reads, sets, or changes a password of an existing user. Creating a
+   missing user requires *some* password for `synouser --add`, so create
+   installs a cryptographically random throwaway (the provision script's
+   proven pattern for the operator account), reports
+   `"password": "unset (owner ceremony required)"`, and the real credential
+   ceremony stays human (DSM UI + household password manager).
+6. **Seam self-preservation** (the design/39 §5 trap): any DSM user-database
+   write regenerates `/etc/passwd` and reverts the seam accounts
+   (`syrvis-operator`, `syrvis-reader`) to `/sbin/nologin` — which would
+   sever the very seam this verb arrived on. After its converge phase,
+   `user declare` (and, as cheap insurance, `share declare`) re-asserts
+   `/bin/sh` on both seam accounts *in the same invocation* — idempotent,
+   guarded, the boot hook's exact self-heal — and reports it under
+   `"seam_selfheal"`. The in-flight SSH session survives regardless (the
+   shell change affects new logins); the self-heal keeps the *next* call
+   alive.
+
+## 6. Backend choices (and why they diverge from the original sketch)
+
+The sketch proposed `synoshare` for shares and `synoacltool` for the ACL
+matrix. The evidence went the other way:
+
+### 6.1 User plane — proven low-level primitives
+
+Every mutation form is one the estate has already executed successfully:
+
+- **Reads**: `/etc/passwd` + `/etc/group` parsing (exactly what the
+  deployment's account checks do today). No parsing of `synouser --get`
+  bracket-text.
+- **Create**: `synouser --add <name> <random-pw> <fullname> 0 "" ""` — the
+  provision script's live-verified form.
+- **Groups**: `synogroup --memberadd <group> <member>` (additive; the
+  provision script's form, with its known caveat that very old DSMs lack
+  `--memberadd` — surfaced as a per-item error, not silently swallowed).
+- **Shell**: surgical awk-equivalent rewrite of the one passwd field, atomic
+  rename, record-count + edit-took safety checks — the provision script's
+  pattern reimplemented in Python.
+
+`synouser --modify` (unreliable across DSM versions) is not used; full-name
+drift is report-only.
+
+### 6.2 Share plane — structured `synowebapi`, not `synoshare` text
+
+There is no proven `synoshare --add`/flag-set form anywhere in the estate
+(the hand ceremony was the DSM **web UI**), `synoshare`'s text output has no
+stable parse contract, and `synoacltool` has zero prior art — while the ACL
+matrix the design actually ratified is DSM **share permissions** (the UI's
+Permissions tab), not filesystem ACEs. So the share backend drives the same
+API the UI drives, on-box as root, exactly like the VMM adapter precedent:
+
+- `synowebapi --exec api=SYNO.Core.Share method=list|get|create|set ...`
+- `synowebapi --exec api=SYNO.Core.Share.Permission method=list|set ...`
+
+JSON in/out, `success`/`error` envelope, one narrow adapter class
+(`DsmShareBackend`) holding every argv form. This is *inside* the audited
+seam (design/51 D8 forbids laptop-side DSM web-API credentials; on-box
+synowebapi under the enumerated sudoers boundary is the D5-sanctioned path).
+
+**Honesty clause**: the exact `SYNO.Core.Share*` parameter shapes are
+asserted from DSM API documentation and community usage, not yet executed
+against a live DSM 7.2 (this change ships with the NAS untouched). They are
+isolated in `DsmShareBackend` (every method carries a `VERIFY-ON-NAS` note),
+and the §3 re-read step means a wrong assumption reports as residual drift —
+loudly — instead of a false convergence. First production use is gated on
+the deployment side: run `share list` (read-only), then
+`share declare --dry-run`, and eyeball both against the registry before the
+first mutating run.
+
+### 6.3 Failure surface
+
+Backend errors raise typed `ShareError`/`UserError` (SyrvisError subclasses,
+stable `code`s) inside the per-item aggregation; the CLI boundary renders the
+`{"error": ...}` envelope for whole-payload failures (bad JSON, schema
+violation, guard refusal) exactly like `apply`.
+
+## 7. The verb catalog — `seam verbs` / `gen verbs`
+
+Contract-polish item (design/51 D5 carries it explicitly): external tooling
+must be able to *read* the seam contract instead of hardcoding it.
+
+- `registry.catalog()` — pure function serializing every `Command` (id, cli,
+  subcommand, tiers, flags/slots, positional, stdin wire format, timeout)
+  plus the payload schemas (§2.3) under `"payloads"`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "catalog_version": 1,
+  "service_version": "<__version__ or null>",
+  "commands": [ {"id": "share_declare", "cli": "syrvis",
+                 "subcommand": ["share", "declare"], "sudo": true,
+                 "read_only": false, "destructive": false,
+                 "stdin": "syrvis-shares/v1", "expect_json": true,
+                 "flags": [{"literal": "--json"}], "positional": null,
+                 "timeout_s": 240}, ... ],
+  "payloads": {"syrvis-shares/v1": {…schema…}, "syrvis-users/v1": {…schema…}}
+}
+```
+
+- **Both binocular ends emit the identical document**:
+  - `python -m syrviscore.seam.gen verbs` — build/CI-side, runnable straight
+    from a source tree with no venv (the generator's existing stdlib-only
+    promise; the ratified drift-check surface for consumers' CI).
+  - `syrvis seam verbs --json` — a registered **read-only, no-sudo** verb, so
+    a deployment can ask the *deployed* instance for its live contract over
+    the seam and diff it against the pinned expectation.
+- `catalog_version` bumps only on a breaking shape change (additive fields do
+  not), mirroring the hostnames-report convention.
+
+## 8. Deliberately out of scope
+
+- **Share/user deletion, user disable/enable, renames, volume moves** —
+  OWNER-DECISION, DSM UI / break-glass only (§5.2).
+- **Passwords, 2FA enrolment, DSM application privileges** (File Station /
+  Photos / WebDAV app grants): human ceremony and UI; the schema has no
+  fields for them, `user list` does not report app privileges.
+- **Encrypted share creation, key custody, mount/unmount** (§5.3).
+- **Filesystem ACEs (`synoacltool`) and per-principal subdirectories** inside
+  shares: the ratified matrix is share-level; subdir conventions belong to
+  the deployment's runbooks.
+- **NFS/AFP/FTP protocol toggles**: service-level DSM settings, asserted by
+  deployment-side checks, not per-share seam state.
+- **MCP tools for the new verbs** (lists are future candidates; declares are
+  stdin writers and stay script-only, per the seam contract).
+- **Client-side rendering** (`shares.d/*.yaml` → payload) and the
+  deployment's share-drift verify check: deployment-repo follow-ups; this
+  spec fixes the wire format they render to.
+- **Release/version bump**: none in this change — packaging is decided at
+  review.
